  "culture": {
    "title": "Citizens, metics and the talk of the agora",
    "md": "Athens in 432 had perhaps 40,000 adult male **citizens** (πολῖται): men born of an Athenian father and, since Pericles' law of 451, an Athenian mother, enrolled in their father's deme at eighteen. Only they voted in the Assembly, sat on juries, owned land and held office. Around them lived the **metics** (μέτοικοι), free foreigners who had settled in Attica under a citizen sponsor: they paid a yearly tax of twelve drachmas, served in the army and paid for festivals, but could not vote, own land or marry a citizen's daughter. Many of the potters and painters of the Kerameikos were metics; names like Lydos ('the Lydian'), Skythes and Brygos on Attic vases say as much. Cephalus of Syracuse, a metic invited by Pericles, ran a shield workshop in Piraeus, and his son Lysias became the finest speech-writer of the next generation. Below both stood the slaves, and beside them the women, who did not take part in public life at all.\n\nThe agora was where a citizen learned the news. Democritus is a type Aristophanes would have recognized: the farmer who comes into town, plants himself under a stoa and tells everyone what he thinks. Young men (νεανίαι) gathered at the barbers' and perfume-sellers' shops; a certain Socrates, thirty-seven years old in 432, was already stopping them with questions. The talk that summer was of war. Corinth and Megara were pressing Sparta to act against Athens, Athens had blockaded Potidaea, and in the autumn the Spartan assembly would vote that the treaty of 446 had been broken. Democritus is wrong that there is a war; he is right that one is coming.",
    "image": "culture-agora-citizens"
  },
